# [Co-developed with claude code -- Adam]
# The P4 proxy's rule journal (KNOWN-ISSUES A-4c, finding #71). Per-machine, per-generation
# state: an append-only JSONL of every rule THIS machine's proxy was asked to install, written
# by proxy_agent/main.py's `build_rule_journal`. Committing it would put one lab's forwarding
# history into everyone's checkout, and `quarantine()` names its predecessors with a boot id and
# a timestamp, so the directory grows a file per proxy generation. Ignored as a directory: the
# quarantined names are `rule_journal.jsonl.<boot>.<epoch>` and no single filename pattern
# covers them.
p4_proxy/.run/

# Investigation working area. 3.3 GB as of 2026-08-19 -- scratch/lab alone holds the
# captured pcaps and switch logs from the live rounds, and none of it is reviewable in a
# diff. Findings worth keeping get promoted to doc/audit/<date>_<topic>/ instead.
scratch/

# [Co-developed with claude code -- Adam]
# Measurement traces are committed gzipped -- the 08-19 round already did
# (doc/audit/2026-08-19_p4-sflow-accuracy/*.jsonl.gz) and the 08-20 round broke that convention
# by committing them plain. A single 300 s trace is 600-1200 JSON lines; a 12-cell matrix is
# 33k lines and 89 MB that diff as pure noise. Commit the .gz, keep the plain file local.
doc/audit/**/raw/*.jsonl
doc/audit/**/raw/*_server.log

# --- audit raw artifacts ------------------------------------------------------------------
# [Co-developed with claude code -- Adam]
# Raw logs and dumps live on the `audit-raw` orphan branch, not on a working branch: they were
# 166k lines of every pull request diff and none of the review. Per-round raw/ directories carry
# their own .gitignore too, but only after someone remembers to add one -- a round created on
# 2026-08-25 did not, and 279k lines went into a commit before the amend.
#
# The line below used to read `raw/*` and claimed, right here, to cover "every future round
# without anyone remembering". It did not. A round on 2026-08-27 wrote to raw_n/, raw_gil/ and
# raw_h/, which `raw/*` does not match, and 166k lines went onto the working branch -- while the
# same round dutifully gzipped its tarballs, so the author clearly was managing volume and was
# simply outside the pattern's literal reach. Widened to `raw*` so a suffix cannot slip past it.
# The claim of completeness is deleted rather than reworded: this rule covers directories whose
# names start with raw, and nothing else.
#
# 🔴 2026-08-31, third instance of the same shape: `*` in a .gitignore pattern does NOT cross
# `/`, so `doc/audit/*/raw*/*` reaches exactly one level. A round that groups its work into a
# sub-directory -- 2026-08-31_completeness-experiments put raw under B-nslab-build/raw/ and
# A-ovs-framesweep/raw_probe/ -- lands one level deeper and is not matched. Both directories
# showed as untracked rather than ignored, which reads as ordinary new work.
# 🔑 The pre-commit gate was NOT blind to this (verified by staging a nested raw file and
# running it: rc=1, refused) because a git PATHSPEC `*` does cross `/` while a gitignore `*`
# does not. Two rules that look identical, written from the same intent, with different
# matching semantics -- so the gate held and the ignore leaked, and only one of them says so.
# `**` is the gitignore spelling that crosses directories; the one-level lines stay because a
# pattern that is merely redundant costs nothing and deleting it would need its own argument.
doc/audit/*/raw*/*
!doc/audit/*/raw*/.gitignore
doc/audit/**/raw*/*
!doc/audit/**/raw*/.gitignore
#
# A pattern here is advice, not a gate: `git add -f` walks straight past it, and 44 commits did
# exactly that. The gate is tools/githooks/pre-commit, which refuses raw on any branch but
# audit-raw. Install it with tools/githooks/install.sh -- an uninstalled hook enforces nothing.
doc/audit/2026-08-25_sampling-rounds/ladder_ext.log
doc/audit/2026-08-25_sampling-rounds/ladder_ext.out
# Ticket G: 245 py-spy dumps, 15 MB uncompressed. The tarball beside it is committed.
# These three used to be listed as directories (`raw_gil/`), which is redundant with the `raw*`
# rule above and had one side effect: ignoring the directory stops git descending into it, so
# the `!.gitignore` exception could never fire and these three rounds were the only ones whose
# raw/ carried no note saying where their artifacts went. Dropped in favour of the general rule.
doc/audit/2026-08-25_sampling-rounds/gil_g.log
doc/audit/2026-08-25_sampling-rounds/h_probe.log
doc/audit/2026-08-25_sampling-rounds/*.log
